<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Banu Ijl</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Banu Ijl were an Arab tribe of the Bakr ibn Wa'il confederation, living in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> and on the edges of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. Clans of the tribe took part in the victory over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanians</a> at Dhi Qar. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a> the tribe produced the Dulafid family, whose founder <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-dulaf">Abu Dulaf</a> governed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jibal">Jibal</a> in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/banu-ijl.jpg" alt="Banu Ijl" width="320" height="349" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: MWahaiibii · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Banu_Ajal_Flag_First_(7).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The tribe, also called the Banu Ajal, traced its descent from Ijl ibn Lujaym, whose line ran back through Bakr ibn Wa'il to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabia">Rabi'a ibn Nizar</a>, Ma'ad and Adnan. Ijl had three sons, Tha'laba, Abd al-Aswad and Yazid, and the tribe was divided into three sub-clans: the Banu Dubay'a, the Banu Sa'd and the Banu Rabi'a.</p><p>The Banu Ijl lived in Lower Mesopotamia, in the lands from Ayn al-Tamr south to the shores around the town of al-Ubulla, and some members lived in al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamama">Yamama</a>. The tribe belonged to al-Lahazim, an alliance with the Banu Qays, the Banu Taym and the Banu Anazzah, formed as a defensive coalition against other tribes, mainly the Banu Yarbu' and the Banu Shayban. With other Arab tribes it fought at Dhi Qar, where the Sasanian army was defeated.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Christians made up a significant part of the tribe. On the eve of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> its leader, Abjar ibn Jabir, was a Christian, and he remained one until his death at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufa">Kufa</a> in 660. In the early Muslim conquests, Muslim tribesmen of the Banu Ijl joined the army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalid-ibn-al-walid">Khalid ibn al-Walid</a> in the battles of Walaja and Ullais. At Walaja, it is reported, they fought against Christian members of their own tribe who had taken the side of the Sasanian army.</p><p>Under the Abbasid caliphs the tribe produced two notable soldiers, both of whom carried its name as al-Ijli. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-dulaf">Abu Dulaf</a> al-Ijli served as a military commander under the caliph al-Mamun. Jahwar ibn Marar al-Ijli commanded troops for the caliph al-Mansur, put down the rebellion of Sunpadh, and later rose in revolt against the caliphate himself.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Banu Ijl” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Abu Dulaf al-Qasim ibn Isa, of the Arab <b>Banu Ijl</b>, was a general and poet in the service of Harun al-Rashid and al-Ma'mun.</blockquote><a href="/state/dulafid-dynasty">Dulafid dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
